sevenz/ - 7-Zip SDK decoder cores for ZIP-style 7z support.
==========================================================

Source
------
7-Zip SDK (ip7z/7zip, `main` branch), PUBLIC DOMAIN (Igor Pavlov),
fetched as raw files and copied verbatim. `LICENSE` is the SDK's
DOC/7zC.txt (LZMA SDK public-domain statement).

Only the *codec cores* are vendored - NOT the container:
`7zArcIn.c`, `7zDec.c`, `7zIn.c`, `7zItem.c` are deliberately NOT
taken (they drag in the SDK allocator/stream machinery); the .7z
container is parsed by our own clean-room `SevenZDecode.cpp` over
these cores, same pattern as `XzDecode`/`LzmaDecode`.

Compiled in (see XacRett.dsp "sevenz" group):
  Aes.c Aes.h Sha256.c Sha256.h   AES-256-CBC + SHA-256
  Bcj2.c Bcj2.h                   BCJ2 decoder
  Bra.c Bra.h Bra86.c             branch converters (Bra.c has no X86;
                                  X86 lives in Bra86.c)
  CpuArch.c CpuArch.h             CPU feature detection
  Delta.c Delta.h                 Delta (coder 03)
  Ppmd7.c Ppmd7.h Ppmd7Dec.c
  Ppmd.h                          PPMdH (coder 04)
Headers only: 7zTypes.h 7zWindows.h Compiler.h Precomp.h
              RotateDefs.h

Not compiled: 7zArcIn.c 7zDec.c 7zIn.c 7zItem.c 7zAlloc.c
              Bcj2Enc.c BraIA64.c Bra86 asm variants, AesOpt.c
              Sha256Opt.c CpuArch asm, and the whole CPP/ tree.

VC6 port shims (2 files, both REAL upstream-gate bugs)
-----------------------------------------------------
Upstream enables hardware paths on a bare `_MSC_VER` test:

  Sha256.c:  `#if ... defined(_MSC_VER) && (_MSC_VER >= 1200)`
  Aes.c:     `#elif defined(_MSC_VER)`

**VC6 reports _MSC_VER == 1200**, so BOTH tests wrongly match, and
the code then references assembly-only symbols
(`Sha256_UpdateBlocks_HW`, `AesCbc_{Encode,Decode}_HW`,
`AesCtr_Code_HW`, `AesCbc_Decode_HW_256`, `AesCtr_Code_HW_256`,
`CPU_IsSupported_{SHA,SSSE3,AES,VAES_AVX2}`) that only exist in
.asm / hardware-only builds -> LNK2001 at link. We narrowed both to
`_MSC_VER > 1200`, which keeps the pure-C paths. Correct for a
1998-era target: no SHA-NI / AES-NI instructions exist.

A second, sharper trap of the same family: `Sha256_Init` stores NULL
into `p->v.vars.func_UpdateBlocks` in the `#else` (no-HW) branch, so
a caller MUST NOT be told to call `Sha256Prepare()`-driven dispatch
expecting a non-null pointer - the direct-call path
(`SHA256_UPDATE_BLOCKS(p)` macro) is what actually runs.

Verification (VC6, in-tree harness `mk7ztest.bat`)
--------------------------------------------------
o SHA-256: 3 NIST vectors exact - "abc", empty, 'a'x1000.
o AES-256-CBC: NIST SP800-38A F.2.5 exact, both directions;
  NIST F.1.5 (ECB, 1 block) exact.
o Delta: output matches a from-spec reference model for delta 1..16.
  NOTE Delta is NOT self-inverse across a single call pair - the
  decoder must start from the same zero state the encoder started
  from (the encoder's FINAL state is the trailing `delta` bytes and
  is NOT the decoder's initial state).
o Bra x86 (ConvSt_X86_Enc/Dec): encode->decode restores input.

Required call order / buffer layout (cost real debugging time)
------------------------------------------------------------
o `Sha256Prepare()` once before any `Sha256_Init`.
o AES: key schedule goes at `Aes()+4`, IV at `Aes()+0`; buffer is
  `UInt32 [AES_NUM_IVMRK_WORDS + 3]`, all 16-byte aligned.
  Decrypt needs `Aes_SetKey_Dec`, encrypt `Aes_SetKey_Enc`.
  (`AesCbc_Init` fills ONLY the IV - it does not touch the schedule.)
